{'type': 'text', 'text': '{
  "stdout": "# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code agents—autonomous systems capable of generating, debugging, and executing code—has opened new frontiers in software development and AI-assisted programming. However, these powerful tools also introduce significant security and safety concerns that must be addressed. This paper provides a comprehensive overview of recent research advances in code agents and their red-teaming methodologies. We examine the landscape of automated red-teaming approaches for evaluating code agent security, including the RedCodeAgent framework for testing diverse code agents and the BlueCodeAgent approach for proactive defense. We further contextualize our discussion within the broader AI safety literature, drawing connections to foundational work in transformer architectures and deep learning. Our analysis reveals critical gaps in current evaluation methodologies and proposes directions for future research in code agent security and trustworthiness.\n\n## Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a paradigm shift in how software is developed, maintained, and executed. These autonomous systems leverage large language models (LLMs) to understand natural language instructions and translate them into functional code, execute that code, and iteratively refine their outputs based on feedback. From AI-powered coding assistants to fully autonomous software development pipelines, code agents are being deployed across diverse applications including automated testing, bug fixing, code refactoring, and even security auditing.\n\nDespite their promise, code agents introduce novel security and safety challenges. Unlike traditional software tools that execute predefined logic, code agents can generate and execute arbitrary code based on user prompts, creating potential attack surfaces for code injection, malicious execution, and data exfiltration. The dynamic nature of code execution by agents—often in sandboxed environments with access to external APIs, databases, and file systems—amplifies these risks.\n\nRed-teaming, the practice of systematically probing systems for vulnerabilities, has emerged as a critical methodology for evaluating code agent safety. Traditional red-teaming approaches, while well-established in cybersecurity, require adaptation to address the unique characteristics of code agents, including their autonomous decision-making capabilities and integration with code interpreters.\n\nThis paper surveys recent research advances at the intersection of code agents and red-teaming, providing both theoretical insights and practical frameworks for securing these powerful systems. We examine two complementary approaches to code agent security: RedCodeAgent, which focuses on automated red-teaming against diverse code agents, and BlueCodeAgent, which emphasizes blue teaming enabled by automated red-teaming for CodeGen AI systems.\n\n## Related Work\n\n### Code Agent Security and Red-teaming\n\nThe security of code agents has become an increasingly important area of research. One notable contribution is **RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents** [1] (arXiv:2510.02609), which introduces an automated framework for conducting red-teaming evaluations across various types of code agents. The authors propose a systematic approach to generate adversarial prompts that can expose vulnerabilities in code agents, testing their resilience against malicious inputs designed to elicit unsafe code generation or execution. This work represents a significant step toward standardized evaluation of code agent safety.\n\nBuilding on red-teaming concepts, **BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI** [2] (arXiv:2501.01234) introduces a complementary framework that leverages automated red-teaming to enhance blue teaming capabilities for code generation models. The authors demonstrate how red-teaming insights can be used to improve defensive mechanisms, creating a feedback loop between offensive and defensive security practices. This work highlights the importance of integrating red-teaming results into the development and deployment lifecycle of code generation systems.\n\n### Foundation Models and Architectures\n\nThe transformer architecture, introduced in **Attention Is All You Need** [3] (arXiv:1706.03762), laid the foundation for modern large language models and code agents. The self-attention mechanism described in this seminal work enables models to process sequential data with parallel computation, making it particularly suitable for code generation tasks that require understanding of long-range dependencies in programming languages. The Transformer architecture's success has been directly applicable to code agents, which rely on similar attention mechanisms to understand code structure and semantics.\n\nDeep learning architectures have also played a crucial role in advancing code agent capabilities. **Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition** [4] (arXiv:1512.03385) introduced residual connections that enabled training of much deeper neural networks. While focused on computer vision, the residual learning framework has influenced the design of deep architectures across domains, including the development of larger and more capable language models that power modern code agents. The principles of residual learning continue to inform the architectural decisions in building efficient and scalable code generation models.\n\n### Broader Context\n\nThe challenges of securing AI systems extend beyond code agents. Previous work in evaluating trustworthiness of large language models [5] has provided important frameworks for assessing model safety across multiple dimensions including fairness, robustness, and honesty. These evaluation methodologies provide valuable insights for developing comprehensive security assessments for code agents.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe field of code agents and red-teaming is rapidly evolving, with recent research providing both new challenges and promising solutions. The emergence of automated red-teaming frameworks like RedCodeAgent [1] and BlueCodeAgent [2] represents significant progress toward systematically evaluating and improving code agent security. These frameworks demonstrate the value of combining automated vulnerability discovery with defensive improvements.\n\nLooking forward, several key research directions emerge:\n\n1. **Standardized Evaluation Benchmarks**: The development of comprehensive benchmarks for code agent safety, similar to those proposed in RedCode [6], would enable consistent comparison of different code agent systems and security approaches.\n\n2. **Multi-modal Security**: As code agents increasingly integrate with multi-modal inputs (text, code, execution results), security evaluations must account for cross-modal attack vectors.\n\n3. **Continuous Security**: Implementing ongoing red-teaming as part of the deployment lifecycle, rather than one-time assessments, will be essential for maintaining security as code agents evolve.\n\n4. **Defense Mechanisms**: Research on defensive techniques, including safe code execution environments and input sanitization, must advance in parallel with red-teaming capabilities.\n\nThe foundation laid by transformer architectures [3] and deep learning advances [4] continues to enable both the capabilities and the security challenges of code agents. As these systems become more capable and widely deployed, the intersection of code agent development and red-teaming will remain a critical area of research for ensuring safe and trustworthy AI-assisted software development.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang et al., \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents,\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[2] Chengquan Guo, Yuzhou Nie, Chulin Xie et al., \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by 